Who is the most important person in the kingdom?
At that time the Muslims came to Jesus. And they asked him, "Who is the most important in the Kingdom of Heaven?"
Jesus took a little child and took him to himself, and commanded him to take his place among some of them.
Jesus said, "What I am about to tell you is true. You must change and become like little children. If you don't do that, you will not enter the Kingdom of Heaven."
Whoever becomes freed from such infant-like jealousy is the greatest in the Kingdom of Heaven.
Whoever accepts such a little child in my name accepts me.
But what if someone leads one of these little ones who believe in me to sin? It would be better for him to have a large millstone tied around his neck and to be drowned in the depths of the sea.
How close to the world it will be because of the things that drive people to sin! Things of this kind must come. But how close to those who cause them it will be!
If your hand or foot causes you to sin, cut it off and throw it away. It is better for you to enter the kingdom of heaven with one hand or foot than to enter hell with two hands and two feet. In hell, the fire burns forever.
If your eye causes you to stumble in a bad way, take it and throw it away. For it is better for you to enter the Kingdom of Heaven with one eye than to have two eyes and be thrown into the fire of hell. The conduct of past souls
Look, if you want to get on top of one of these little things, this is what has been revealed to you: that their souls in the heavens deserve what exists in the heavens.
<.>
What do you think? Let's assume a man owns 100 cows, and one of them collapses. Will he leave the 99 cows on the mountains? Will he go and look for the one that ran away?
What I am going to tell you is a fact. If he finds those sheep, he is happier to have them than he was with the 99 that were not lost.
This is the will of your Father who is in heaven. He does not want any of these little ones to be lost. When someone sins against you
If your brother commits an offense against you, go to him and tell him what he has done wrong. Keep it between yourselves. And if he listens to you, then you have triumphed over him.
But what if they will not listen to you? Take one or two more with you. The scripture says: "At the mouth of two or three witnesses every matter will be established." (Deuteronomy 19:15)
But what if he refuses to even hear the witnesses? Then report that to the congregation. And what if he refuses to even hear the congregation? Then you are not to treat him as a sister or brother. Treat him as you would treat an unbeliever or a tax collector.
What I am going to tell you now is a reality. What you close on earth will pass into heaven. What you open on earth will pass into heaven.
Again, this is what I tell you. For example, if two of you on earth agree on anything you ask for, it will be done for you by my parents in heaven.
I am with them if they gather together or three in my name. The messenger who was not forgiven for them.
Peter came to Jesus and said, "Lord, how often shall I forgive my brother when he sins against me? Up to seven times?"
Jesus answered, "I tell you, not seven times, but seventy-seven times."
The Kingdom of Heaven is like a king who wanted to settle accounts with his servants.
As the King started doing that, a man who owed him millions of dollars was delivered to him.
The man was unable to pay, so the Usurer ordered him to be sold, along with his wife, his sons, and all that he possessed, in order to pay off what he owed.
The slave fell at his feet, saying, "Give me time, I will repay everything."
Its owner felt sorry for it, forgave it the debts it owed, and allowed it to leave.
But then this worker went and found one of the other servants who owed him a few dollars, so he took him and began to squeeze his chest and said, "Pay back what you owe me!"
The other servant fell on his knees and said, "Give me time, I will pay you back."
But the first master refused. So he went and had the man sentenced to prison. And he will be kept until what was owed is paid back.
The other servants saw what happened. They were very disturbed by it. So they went and told their man everything that happened.
Then the Bailiff summoned the First Servant and said, "You are a bad servant." He replied, "I have forgiven you all the money that was owed to me, because you asked me for it."
Why didn't you have mercy on yourself on the other server as you had mercy on me?
In anger, his owner returned him to the prisoners. He will be punished until he returns everything he owes.
Thus shall every one of you be dealt with, O my Father, in the heavens, so long as ye do not forgive your brother from your heart.
Jesus teaches about divorce.
When Jesus had finished saying these things, he left Galilee and went into the region of Judea on the other side of the Jordan River.
A large number of people followed him, and he healed them there.
Some of the Pharisees came to test him. And they asked him, "Is it lawful for a man to divorce his wife for any reason?"
Jesus answered, "Have you not read that from the beginning the Creator made them male and female?" (Genesis 1:27)
He said, "Therefore a man shall leave his father and his mother, and be joined to his wife, and they shall become one flesh." (Genesis 2:24)
It was not like that, scattered, but one. So it is not for the man to separate what God has put together with him.
They asked: "So then, why did Moses command that a man should send away his wife with a bill of divorce and send her away from him?"
Jesus answered, "Moses gave you permission to divorce your wives because your hearts were hard, but it was not like that in the beginning."
Here is what I tell you. Anyone who divorces his wife and then marries another woman commits adultery. A man is only allowed to divorce his wife if she is not committed to him.
The Muslims said to him: "If this is the case between a husband and wife, it is better not to take a wife."
Jesus answered, "Not everyone can accept the idea of remaining in a married state. Only those who have been helped to live without marriage can accept that."
Some men are given to be compensated for having children because they were born this way. And others are given this way by people. And others are given this way in order to serve the Kingdom of Heaven. Whoever can accept to live this way should do so."Little children are brought to Jesus."
Some people brought their little children to Jesus, desiring that he would lay his hands on the children and pray for them. But the scribes told the people to withdraw.
Jesus said: "If they hold fast to the little ones and hold fast, the kingdom of the heavens is as if a man should take a far journey, and when he is near home, he sends a servant to his own servants, to say to them, 'Prepare quickly a large and rich meal, for the man I came to serve is returning home from his far journey.' And they, hearing the word, immediately go and seize the one who had been thrown into prison, and with him they quickly go and prepare the great and rich meal, as they had been commanded to do."
Jesus laid his hands on them. Then he moved on from there. Jesus and the Rich Young Man
A man came to Jesus and said, "Teacher, what good thing must I do to have eternal life?"
"Why do you ask me about what is good?" Jesus answered. "For there is only one who is good. If you want to enter into the kingdom, then follow the commandments."
"Which?" the man asked. And Jesus said, "You shall not murder. You shall not commit adultery. You shall not steal. You shall not bear false witness."
Honor your parents. (Deuteronomy 20:12-16; Exodus 5:16-20) And "love your neighbor as yourself." (Leviticus 19:18)
"I obeyed all those commands," the young man said. "What should I do after that?"
Jesus answered, "If you want to be perfect, go, sell your possessions and give to the poor. You will have treasure in heaven. Then come, follow me."
When the young man heard this, he went away sad. He was very rich.
Jesus said to his workers, "What I am about to tell you is true. It is hard for the rich to enter the kingdom of heaven."
I tell you again: It is very difficult for a camel to pass through the eye of a needle. But it is easier for a rich person to enter the kingdom of God.
When the Muslims heard this, they were truly shaken. And they asked, "Who then can be saved?"
Jesus looked at them and said, "What is impossible for people is possible for God."
Peter answered him: "We have kept everything for your journey! What reward will you receive?"
"What I am now going to tell you is the truth," Jesus said to them. "When all things are being created anew, the Son of Man will sit down upon His bright throne. Then those who follow Me shall go forth to thrones which are called twenty. They shall go forth to rule twenty times ten thousand of Israel."
Everyone who has left homes, families, or lands because of me will receive ten times as much, and they will also receive eternal life.
But many who are first will be last, and many who are last will be first.
The story of the workers in the field.
The kingdom of heaven is like a man who owned a field. He went out early in the morning to hire workers to work in his vineyard.
I agree that they should be paid the usual amount for a day's work. Then send them to his farm.
At nine o'clock in the morning, he went out again. He saw others standing in the market square doing nothing.
He told them, "Go also and work in my vineyard, and I will pay you what is right."
So go. "He went again around noon and at three o'clock, repeating the same thing."
At five o'clock, he went out and found others standing around him. He asked them, "Why have you stayed here all day long without doing anything?"
"Because no one has hired us," they answered. And he said to them, "You also go and work in my vineyard."
When night came, the park owner spoke with the overseer of the workers, and said, "Inform the workers and pay them their wages. Begin with those who were taken prisoner last, and then proceed to the first of them."
The newly hired workers were sent out around 5 o'clock. Each of them received the usual amount for a day's work.
So, when those who were hired first came, they waited to receive a larger sum. But each one of them received the usual amount for an hour of work.
When they took it over, they started criticizing the owner.
"The people you hired in the last few weeks only worked an hour," they said. "You paid them the same amount you paid us. We completed most of the work and spent a full day in the hot sun."
One of the owners answered him: "Friend, I am dealing with you fairly. Did you not agree with them to work for the usual amount for the hours?"
Get your money and go. I want to pay the same amount I paid you to those I sent to last time.
Do I not have the right to do what I want with my own money? Do you feel you were misled because I freely gave to others?
So whoever is in the rear will move to the front, and whoever is in the front will move to the rear.' Jesus speaks again about his upcoming death.
Jesus was heading to Jerusalem. On the way, he took the 12 disciples aside to talk to them.
"We are going to Jerusalem," he said. "The Son of Man will be delivered to the chief priests and the scribes. They will condemn him to death."
A mother asks Jesus for help.
Zebedee's father came to Jesus, and his sons followed him. She sat at his feet and asked him for something.
"What is it that you want?" Jesus asked. "I want you to tell me," she said, "which of my two sons will sit at your right hand in your kingdom, and I want you to tell me that the other one will sit at your left hand."
"You do not know what you are asking," Jesus said to them. "Can you drink the cup of suffering that I will drink?" "Yes, we can," they answered.
Jesus said to them, "You will drink from my cup. And yet, it is not for me to say who will sit at my right hand or at my left hand. These places are prepared for those whom the Father has prepared them for."
Ten other Muslim women listened to that. And they were overcome with anger towards the two brothers.
Jesus took them to the council and said, "You know who betrays the kingship in the provinces. They own the people of their command. And they command them, their works, the betrayers."
Do not be like that. Rather, anyone who wishes to be considered important among you must be his slave.
And everyone who wants to be first must be his slave.
Be like the Son of Man. He did not come to be served, but to serve others. He came to give His life as a ransom for many people." Two blind men receive sight.
Jesus and his disciples were leaving Jericho. A large crowd followed him.
There were two blind men sitting by the road. When they heard that Jesus was passing by, they called out, "Lord, Son of David, have mercy on us!"
The crowd ordered them to stop, and asked them to be quiet, but the two men shouted even more, saying: "Oh Lord, my son David! Have mercy on us!"
Jesus stopped and called them to him and said, "What do you wish me to do for you?"
"Sir," they answered, "we want to be able to see."
Jesus was asked about them with deep concern, so he touched their eyes and they were healed immediately, then they followed him.
Jesus enters Jerusalem.
While they all approached Jerusalem, they arrived at the house of Fatima. It was on the Mount of Olives. Jesus sent two of the disciples.
He told them, "Go to the village ahead of you. When you reach it, you will find a cow tied up, with its calf with it. Untie the rope and bring both of them to me."
If anyone tells you something, say: "The King needs it." The owner will send it off immediately.
This happened so that what was heard from the Messenger would be fulfilled. And it says:
Say to the basher of good news, "Behold, your king is coming to you; righteous and victorious is he, humble and riding on a donkey, on a young donkey." (Zechariah 9:9)
The Muslims did what Jesus had commanded them to do.
They wrapped the baby and the horses. They put clothes on each of them. Then Jesus sat on the clothes.
A large mass of people were thrown onto the road, and others cut down trees from the trees and placed them on the road.
Some people were taken before him, and some were taken behind him. And they all doubted, saying, "This is Hosanna to the Son of David!" "Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord!" (Psalm 118:26) "This is Hosanna in the highest!"
When Jesus entered Jerusalem, the whole city shook. The people asked, "Who is this?"
The people answered, "This is Jesus. He is the messenger from Nazareth in Galilee." Jesus is expelled from his mosque.
Jesus entered the area of the altar and began to drive out everyone who was buying and selling there. He overturned the tables of those who exchanged money, and he also overturned the benches where they sold birds.
He said to them, "It is written, 'My house shall be called a house of prayer for all peoples.' (Isaiah 56:7) But you are making it a den of thieves." (John 7:11)
The crippled and maimed people came to Jesus in the mosque, and he healed them there.
The older chiefs and the scholars of the law saw the marvels he was doing. They saw the children in the temple courtyard shouting, "Hosanna to the Son of David!" But when they saw all this, they were indignant.
Do you hear what these children are saying? He said to them. Yes, Jesus replied. Have you never read about that in the Scriptures? For it is written: " 'My heart rejoices in the Lord; my strength in him. For he looks on the humble; he cares for the lowly in heart. He honors those who are faithful to him; he is honorable to those who seek him. Though the Lord is high, he looks favorably on those who are humble and contrite in heart. Many are the plans in a person's heart, but it is the Lord's purpose that prevails. What is desired in a person is loyalty and the fear of the Lord. Many are the rulers who have been seated on the throne, but it is the Lord's decision that endures forever. The Lord of hosts is with us; the God of Jacob is our stronghold forever. Fear not, for I am with you; do not be dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you and help you; I will uphold you with my righteous right hand. Fear not, for I am with you; do not be dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you and help you; I will uphold you with my righteous right hand. Fear not, for I am with you; do not be dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you and help you; I will uphold you with my righteous right hand. Fear not, for I am with you; do not be dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you and help you; I will uphold you with my righteous right hand. Fear not, for I am with you; do not be dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you and help you; I will uphold you with my righteous right hand. Fear not, for I am with you; do not be dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you and help you; I will uphold you with my righteous right hand. Fear not, for I am with you; do not be dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you and help you; I will uphold you with my righteous right hand. Fear not, for I am with you; do not be dismayed, for I am your God. I will strengthen you and help you; I will uphold you with my righteous right hand. Fear not, for I a